Answer: Ans: start() is a method of the Thread class, and a class gets it only by inheriting from Thread. A class that implements Runnable has just the run() method and does not inherit start(), so calling start() on its object is a compile-time error.

```java
class PrintingNumbers implements Runnable {
public void run() {
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println(i);
}
}
}
public class StartError {
public static void main(String[] args) {
PrintingNumbers pn = new PrintingNumbers();
pn.start();
}
}
```

Sample Input: No input Sample Output: StartError.java:11: error: cannot find symbol symbol: method start() location: variable pn of type PrintingNumbers 1 error The object of PrintingNumbers is not a thread. A Thread object is needed to run it.

Answer: Ans: A Thread object is created directly and the Runnable object is passed to its constructor. Thread is an ordinary class, so its object can be created directly, in the same way as objects of String, StringBuffer, StringBuilder and Scanner. For example, Thread t1 = new Thread(pc); creates a thread for the Runnable object referred to by pc. The class name Thread starts with a capital letter, as class names always do. If the first option, an object of a class that extends Thread, is not possible, this second option is used. Q8. Write a Java program that creates two threads by implementing the Runnable interface, one printing characters and the other printing numbers. Ans: PrintingCharacters and PrintingNumbers implement Runnable, and each holds its independent instructions in run(). In main, an object of each class is created, a Thread object is created for each of them, and start() is called on the Thread objects.

```java
class PrintingCharacters implements Runnable {
public void run() {
for (char ch = 'A'; ch <= 'E'; ch++) {
System.out.println(ch);
}
}
}
class PrintingNumbers implements Runnable {
public void run() {
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println(i);
}
}
}
public class RunnableDemo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
PrintingCharacters pc = new PrintingCharacters();
PrintingNumbers pn = new PrintingNumbers();
Thread t1 = new Thread(pc);
Thread t2 = new Thread(pn);
t1.start();
t2.start();
}
}
```

Sample Input: No input Sample Output (one possible run): A B C D E 1 2 3 4 5 The two threads run concurrently, so the lines of the characters and the numbers can appear in a different order, or mixed together, in different runs. The order inside each thread does not change: the characters always come as A to E and the numbers as 1 to 5.

Answer: Ans: In a program that has only one thread, an unhandled exception terminates the program abruptly. When there are several threads, an exception that occurs in one thread terminates only that thread. The other threads continue to work and execute, because threads are independent of each other. Each thread has its own stack and its own independent instructions. Q13. Write a program in which one Runnable thread throws an exception by dividing by zero while another thread prints numbers. Ans: DivisionTask divides num1 by num2, which is 0, so run() throws an ArithmeticException in its thread. PrintingNumbers is in another thread and is not affected.

```java
class DivisionTask implements Runnable {
public void run() {
int num1 = 10;
int num2 = 0;
int result = num1 / num2;
System.out.println(result);
}
}
class PrintingNumbers implements Runnable {
public void run() {
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
System.out.println(i);
}
}
}
public class ExceptionDemo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Thread t1 = new Thread(new DivisionTask());
Thread t2 = new Thread(new PrintingNumbers());
t1.start();
t2.start();
}
}
```

Sample Input: No input Sample Output (one possible run): Exception in thread "Thread-0" java.lang.ArithmeticException: / by zero at DivisionTask.run(ExceptionDemo.java:5) 1 2 3 4 5 The stack trace continues with lines of the JDK classes, which depend on the Java version, and the position of the exception message among the numbers can differ between runs. The thread running DivisionTask dies, and the thread that prints numbers still prints all its numbers from 1 to 5.
